From: kipclip ops <ops@kipclip.com>
Subject: [PATCH] cmd/tap: saturate backoff exponent before shifting

The TAP resync backoff computed `1 << retries` BEFORE applying the cap.
For large retry counts the integer shift overflows (on 64-bit ints,
shifts >= 63 produce 0 or negative values), which collapses the intended
1-60 minute backoff into a near-now retry storm — the observed
production failure: retry counts of 15k-162k with retry timestamps only
seconds in the future.

Fix: cap the exponent at 60 before shifting, then apply the cap. The
base delay stays within [1, max] seconds plus <=1s jitter, and
retry_after can never drop below ~1s.

BASE: 41278964ec8e3253e70d4e919dfb8e34211c543d (upstream main,
      2026-09-03 — the file on which this patch applies, verified
      byte-identical at patch time; upstream has NO fix for this as of
      2026-09-11: the last commit touching cmd/tap/util.go is
      1d6a130c35cd, 2026-02-03).

STATUS: downstream patch owned by this repository. NOT an upstream
Indigo commit and never pushed to bluesky-social/indigo. Re-apply with
`git apply <patch>` after checkout of BASE; verify clean apply before
building.

---
diff --git a/cmd/tap/util.go b/cmd/tap/util.go
index 4d1d08ff..a1a0f2ff 100644
--- a/cmd/tap/util.go
+++ b/cmd/tap/util.go
@@ -24,9 +24,18 @@
 func backoff(retries int, max int) time.Duration {
-	dur := 1 << retries
-	if dur > max {
-		dur = max
+	// Cap the exponent BEFORE shifting: `1 << retries` overflows for large
+	// retry counts (on 64-bit ints, shifts >= 63 produce 0 or negative values),
+	// which collapsed the intended backoff into a near-now retry storm. With
+	// the exponent capped at 60, the base delay stays within [1, max] seconds
+	// plus <=1s jitter, and retry_after can never drop below ~1s.
+	exp := retries
+	if exp > 60 {
+		exp = 60
-	}
+	}
+	dur := int64(1) << exp
+	if dur > int64(max) {
+		dur = int64(max)
+	}
-
+
-	jitter := time.Millisecond * time.Duration(rand.Intn(1000))
+	jitter := time.Millisecond * time.Duration(rand.Intn(1000))
-	return time.Second*time.Duration(dur) + jitter
+	return time.Second*time.Duration(dur) + jitter
 }
